      "type": "writeup",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1007,
        "body_ja": "",
        "body": "<p>いずれ is a formal adverb commonly used as a part of polite language with the same meaning as words like 何（なに） 'what', どう 'how', どちら 'where', etc. When combined with the adverbial particle も, いずれも may be translated as 'any (A)', or 'all of (A)'. However, the more literal translation is 'how even (A)', 'where even (A)', or 'what even (A)'.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155324'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155325'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155326'></ul>\r\n\r\n<section class='caution'><h4>Caution</h4>\r\n<p>いずれ may be seen in the kanji forms of either 何（いず）れ or 孰（いず）れ, with the latter being far less common, but still relatively easy to come across in literature.</p></section>\r\n\r\n<section class='funfact'><h4>Fun Fact</h4>\r\n<p>いずれにしても, いずれにせよ, and いずれにしろ are all fairly common set-phrases that can be translated as 'in the end', 'at any rate', or 'in any case'. The more literal translation for this particular pattern is 'whatsoever comes about' and is used to express that some particular result cannot be avoided.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184644'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184645'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184646'></ul></section>"
      }
    }
